Code Search is a tool for indexing and then performing
regular expression searches over large bodies of source code.
It is a set of programs written in Go: a web app for searching
and browsing your repositories, and command-line tools.

For background on the trigram index, see
http://swtch.com/~rsc/regexp/regexp4.html.

To install:

	go install github.com/google/codesearch/cmd/...@latest

or, from a checkout:

	go install ./cmd/...


Web app (ND Code Search) quick start
------------------------------------

1. Create a configuration file listing the folders to search:

	mkdir -p ~/.config/codesearch
	cp codesearch.example.yaml ~/.config/codesearch/config.yaml
	$EDITOR ~/.config/codesearch/config.yaml

   The file sets the folders to index (repos), what to exclude,
   and where the index is stored (index_dir). See
   codesearch.example.yaml for every option.

2. Build the index (optional: csweb also does this in the background):

	csindex

3. Start the web app and open http://localhost:2473/

	csweb

The first index run reads every file. Later runs (csindex, or csweb
every update_interval) only scan the folders' metadata and re-read files
whose size or modification time changed, so they take seconds even for
trees as large as AOSP. Use csindex -status to see what is indexed and
csindex -full to rebuild from scratch.


Searching
---------

	foo bar         files containing both foo and bar (regular expressions)
	foo OR bar      files containing either
	-foo, NOT foo   files not containing foo
	(a OR b) -c     grouping; file: and lang: work inside groups too
	"foo(bar"       literal text
	file:\.cc$      path matches a regexp (-file: to exclude; also f:)
	lang:java       language (c, c++, java, kotlin, go, py, rust, ts, bp, aidl, ...)
	repo:aosp       repository name (-repo: to exclude)
	sym:Activity    definitions of a symbol
	case:yes        case-sensitive (the default is case-insensitive;
	                case:auto is case-sensitive only for terms with
	                upper-case letters)

In the code browser, click any identifier to see its definitions and
references, or Ctrl/Cmd-click to jump straight to the definition.
Click line numbers to link to lines (shift-click for ranges).
For files in git repositories, Blame (b) shows who last changed each
line and History (h) lists the commits that changed the file, with
diffs and links to view the file as of each commit. The sidebar's
Outline tab (o) lists the file's classes, functions and methods.
In search results, the sidebar narrows results by repository, language
and directory, and j/k move between matches.
Press / to search, ? for help.

The indexer honors .gitignore files (and .git/info/exclude) in the
indexed folders, including nested repositories; set use_gitignore: false
in the config to index ignored files too.

Cross references are search based: they use the trigram index to find
the identifier as a whole word and classify each hit as a definition or
a reference with language-aware heuristics. That works for every
language without building the code, at the cost of the occasional
false positive.


Command-line tools
------------------

	csindex   build and incrementally update the index from the config file
	csweb     web interface (also keeps the index up to date)
	cindex    the original single-file indexer ($CSEARCHINDEX)
	csearch   search the cindex index from the command line
	cgrep     grep using the same regexp engine, without an index

